{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791547940,"nonce":"0PxA7fppR9BjiClnlCC2Fc2xymE=","message":"Audit at 0ff52c22e687b4a453f75442ba9ccbe4e738e1c4: optimized HIR sharing, copies, and rebuilding\n\nThe runtime uses OptimizedBodies -\u003e Arc\u003cOnceLock\u003cArc\u003cHirFunction\u003e\u003e\u003e. A filled-cell lookup and compile_at_tier share Arcs; they do not deep-clone the body or rerun lowering/type checking. Cranelift codegen borrows the function. Module release/inlining facts are cached per module. LazyModule::function returns an owned decoded body on each request, not a cached decoded Arc.\n\nDeep copies remain:\n- Scratch::reach obtains/decodes/clones the original lowered body before looking for a cached optimized program body. With a cache hit it discards that original and clones the optimized body too. Resolve the cache first.\n- Callee scheduling in compile_lazy_function clones an in-memory body or decodes a snapshot body solely to scan calls, before the body is loaded again for compilation. Borrow in-memory functions. Split-image direct_callees includes address-taken/closure targets, so using that directory unchanged would violate the existing finished-library policy that avoids warming functions merely placed in a table.\n- optimize_body clones the fully optimized scratch function to publish its Arc, leaving an owned copy in the scratch until the batch drains. Scratch entries currently own HirFunction rather than supporting shared, read-only callees with copying on mutation.\n- run_module_recursive clones every function before checking size, eligibility, or whether it has self calls. The main pipeline invokes this pass twice; a nonrecursive operator main gets two unnecessary full-body copies.\n- OSR resumable clones the full function; outline then copies the reachable blocks and values into a separate helper. LLVM single-function wrapping also copies the root and metadata, though LLVM was disabled in the current runs.\n\nRebuilding is possible by design. With LLVM disabled, compilation releases a large non-inlineable optimized body; draining the queue releases scratch modules. Asking for the optimized body later then runs the main pipeline again. body_retention explicitly permits a second pipeline run following eviction. With LLVM enabled, the optimized cell remains through promotion; eligible loop bodies are retained for later resume points. This is not a global optimize-once lifetime guarantee.\n\nNUC diagnostic confirmation, frozen 0ff52c22 binary, LLVM disabled, CPU0, no Cargo/rustc observed, unchanged binary hash:\n- op_overload 100K: one pipeline started; the process ended before the compile worker completed its batch.\n- op_overload 10M once: three pipeline starts, main/anon/new each compiled once.\n- op_overload 10M three times in the same process: still three pipeline starts, main/anon/new each compiled once; checksum 210000000 on all three calls.\nNo duplicate function compilations were observed. This kernel does not show repeated full optimization on each main invocation. The copying is first-call/compiler work; these traces do not establish its allocator time share or a speed improvement. The trace timers include diagnostic overhead and are not benchmark samples. Lua os.clock is an elapsed wall timer here, as tracked by 7a9746f8a8c68001e4c66421f16ba09d7c60947936ae42639f9116bfaf46f957.\n\nEvidence: NUC /tmp/zyntax-mir-sharing-audit-20261009/{report.json,short.stderr.log,long.stderr.log,repeated.stderr.log}; local /private/tmp/zyntax-mir-sharing-audit-20261009.json and /private/tmp/zyntax-mir-sharing-audit.py. Repository code and the existing user files were left unchanged.","files":null}]}